# TEXT: string ordering. Byte-lexicographic over the UNSIGNED byte domain.
# "Z" sorts below "a"; a prefix sorts below its extension; and the non-ASCII
# row is the one that discriminates: "é" is 0xC3 0xA9, "z" is 0x7A, so 0xC3
# (195) sorts ABOVE 0x7A (122) and `t < u` is FALSE. The answer is `1 1 0`.
#
# THIS ORACLE WAS CORRECTED FROM `1 1 1`, 2026-08-23. `1 1 1` requires 0xC3 to
# compare BELOW 0x7A, which is exactly what a SIGNED char comparison does
# (0xC3 read as int8 is -61). It was never an independent answer: e4bc334c,
# the commit that introduced this file, says the numerics/text pages were
# "read off the compiler", so the line transcribed the then-signed behaviour
# while the prose directly above it called that behaviour the defect. The
# oracle and its own header contradicted each other from the first commit,
# and the line — not the compiler — is the half that was wrong.
#
# THE UNSIGNED DOMAIN IS NOT INFERRED FROM THE COMPILER. `str/bytes.id`, an
# oracle in this same corpus that AGREES on both realizations, records the
# bytes of "é" as `2 195 169` — 0xC3 IS 195. An ordering over that same byte
# domain cannot then place 195 below 122; the two oracles cannot both stand.
# `law.md` §0 and §8 make ordinary Lua meaning the entry of the specialization
# chain, and Lua 5.4 §3.4.4 orders strings by C-locale collation, which is
# unsigned-char order. Codepoint order answers `1 1 0` too (U+00E9 > U+007A),
# so every defensible reading agrees and only the signed one does not.
#
# `§12 B-11`, cited by the retired header, does not exist in either tree — the
# same phantom-law family `cbackend/modsign.id` records for `§12 B-4`.
# expect: 1 1 0
main: i64 = ()
  p = "Z"
  q = "a"
  a = 0
  if p < q
    a = 1
  r = "ab"
  s = "abc"
  b = 0
  if r < s
    b = 1
  t = "é"
  u = "z"
  c = 0
  if t < u
    c = 1
  print("{a} {b} {c}")
  0
